<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Víctor Paz Estenssoro</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1907">1907</a>–<a href="/years/2001">2001</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Víctor Paz Estenssoro was a Bolivian statesman who founded the Revolutionary Nationalist Movement (MNR) and served as president four times. Leading the 1952 revolution, he introduced universal suffrage, nationalised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tin">tin</a> mines and began land reform. The military overthrew him in 1964. In his last term, from 1985 to 1989, he ended hyperinflation with a harsh free-market stabilisation programme.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/victor-paz-estenssoro.jpg" alt="Víctor Paz Estenssoro" width="320" height="516" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author, Carlos Mesa collection, La Paz · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:V%C3%ADctor_Paz_Estenssoro_(1964)_(Non-Sepia).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Paz Estenssoro was born on 2 October 1907 to a landowning family in Tarija; his father was a banker. He spent his childhood in Tarija and Oruro and studied economics and law at the Higher University of San Andrés in La Paz, receiving a law degree in 1927. He began work at the Ministry of Finance in 1932, fought in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaco-war">Chaco War</a>, where he reached the rank of artillery sergeant, and started a teaching career in 1939.</p><p>In 1941 he co-founded the Movimiento Nacionalista Revolucionario with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hernan-siles-zuazo">Hernán Siles Zuazo</a> and others. He became finance minister under Colonel Gualberto Villarroel but was forced out and went into exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a>. He ran for president in 1947, finishing third, and again in 1951, when the MNR won although suffrage was limited to a small, propertied part of the population. The government of Mamerto Urriolagoitía annulled the result, and the MNR came to power after a popular revolution the next year.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>In his first government, from 1952 to 1956, Paz Estenssoro extended the vote to all adult citizens, natives and illiterates included, nationalised the largest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tin">tin</a>-mining concerns and began a land distribution programme. Much of the old military was dismantled and reorganised as an arm of the party, while armed workers and peasants, overseen largely by Juan Lechín of the Bolivian Workers' Center, became a counterweight. Barred from a consecutive term, he served as ambassador in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> while Siles governed, then won the 1960 election with Lechín as his running mate.</p><p>The second administration saw dissent and political violence. Paz Estenssoro leaned on the reconstituted army, expelled Lechín and amended the constitution to run again in 1964, choosing General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rene-barrientos">René Barrientos</a> as his running mate. On 4 November 1964 Barrientos and Alfredo Ovando led a coup that overthrew him, and he went into a long exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>He backed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hugo-banzer">Hugo Banzer</a>'s coup of 1971, but Banzer broke with the MNR in 1974 and exiled him, and he taught history at the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/los-angeles">Los Angeles</a> and the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-mexico">New Mexico</a>. In 1985 he came second to Banzer, yet Congress elected him because no candidate had 50 percent. Through Decree 21060 he curbed hyperinflation: unions were repressed, 30,000 miners were removed from state payrolls, and a curfew and mass arrests of union leaders followed. The New Economic Policy, run largely by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gonzalo-sanchez-de-lozada">Gonzalo Sánchez de Lozada</a>, turned a statist economy towards privatisation, though poverty persisted. He retired in 1989 and died on 7 June 2001, after complications from surgery on his leg.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Víctor Paz Estenssoro” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Revolutionary Nationalist Movement, which came to power in the revolution of April 1952 under <b>Víctor Paz Estenssoro</b>, introduced universal suffrage, nationalised the mines and carried out land reform in 1953.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-bolivia">Republic of Bolivia · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Abroad, <b>Víctor Paz Estenssoro</b>’s Revolutionary Nationalist Movement in Bolivia and Carlos Ibáñez del Campo in Chile identified with Peronism.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/peronism">Peronism · Spread &amp; Variants</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Tarija, in what is now <a href="/countries/bolivia">Bolivia</a>. Died in Tarija, in what is now <a href="/countries/bolivia">Bolivia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-21.53,-64.73&amp;year=1968" aria-label="Tarija on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2812 2959 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Víctor Paz Estenssoro"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3262 3229h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3262 3229h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-21.53,-64.73&amp;year=1968">What was here in 1968? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
